diff --git a/docs/on-screen-keyboard.md b/docs/on-screen-keyboard.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..11a15bb3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/on-screen-keyboard.md
@@ -0,0 +1,172 @@
+# On-screen keyboard
+
+The two-step splash plugin can display a touch keyboard while asking for a
+password or an ordinary text answer. It appears automatically when a mapped
+touchscreen is present and no typing keyboard is attached. With a physical
+keyboard attached, touching the screen reveals it; that first touch does not
+type a key. Selection happens on release. Dragging out of a key, cancellation,
+device removal, or leaving the prompt cancels the pending selection.
+
+Build with `-Dtouchscreen=enabled` to require libinput and udev, `auto` to use
+them when available, or `disabled` to omit touchscreen support. Physical input
+does not depend on this option. The input provider, XKB layout model, geometry
+and widget are reusable library components; two-step is the first consumer.
+Script themes do not gain a keyboard automatically.
+
+## Keyboard configuration and input
+
+The widget uses the device manager's configured XKB keymap, including its model,
+layouts, variants and options. Distributions must include XKB keyboard data and
+the desired compose tables in the initramfs and supply XKB settings in
+`/etc/vconsole.conf`, for example:
+
+```
+XKBMODEL=pc105
+XKBLAYOUT=us,de,fr
+XKBVARIANT=,,
+XKBOPTIONS=
+```
+
+Use the same configuration for physical and software input. A legacy `KEYMAP`
+setting alone does not necessarily identify an XKB map. If Plymouth has no XKB
+map, the widget is unavailable; it does not silently assume US input. The daemon
+needs a UTF-8 locale for international input, and the initramfs font must contain
+the corresponding glyphs.
+
+The layout key cycles through configured XKB groups. Shift and AltGr latch until
+the next ordinary key; touching them again cancels the latch. Caps uses XKB lock
+semantics. Software modifier state is independent of physical key presses, so a
+latched touch modifier does not change a physical keyboard's input. The keyboard
+shows its own active layout and Caps state. The existing hardware indicators
+continue to describe physical input.
+
+Dead keys and XKB compose sequences produce UTF-8 text. Pending composition and
+latched modifiers clear when the prompt is dismissed. No separate password
+buffer is maintained by the widget: text goes through Plymouth's existing
+input, backspace and submission handlers. The new software-input entrypoint
+rejects malformed text before changing the line and does not log input bytes.
+Existing physical-input debugging options still have their existing behavior.
+
+The default geometry assumes the usual ISO modifier positions. When XKB options
+remap a modifier to a different role, a custom geometry can choose the matching
+`Behavior` (for example, `latch` for Caps remapped to Control). Action labels
+follow the configured keysym rather than assuming a key name implies its action.
+
+The initial implementation does not provide a candidate-selection IME,
+handwriting, prediction, clipboard input, or press-and-hold repetition. Installing
+a language's XKB map is not equivalent to implementing an IME for that language.
+
+## Touchscreen mapping
+
+Libinput supplies device calibration and normalized coordinates. The widget
+then applies the display's rotation and logical scale before hit testing.
+
+With one display, an unmapped touchscreen uses that display. With multiple
+displays, configure the input device's `WL_OUTPUT` udev property to a DRM
+connector name, for example `eDP-1`. A card-qualified name such as
+`card0-eDP-1` disambiguates matching connector names on multiple GPUs. The
+renderer checks all connectors belonging to a logical head, including cloned
+and tiled outputs. An absent or ambiguous association is not guessed from
+resolution; the corresponding touchscreen will not type until its association
+is unambiguous.
+
+For firmware input devices which claim to be keyboards but cannot be used for
+typing, distributions can set `PLYMOUTH_IGNORE_KEYBOARD=1`. This changes only
+automatic touch-keyboard visibility, not physical event routing. Device-specific
+rules belong to the distribution, not to Plymouth's widget. Include relevant
+rules and touchscreen drivers in the initramfs.
+
+## Appearance
+
+Add an `[OnScreenKeyboard]` group to the theme's `.plymouth` file. Colors are
+24-bit `0xRRGGBB`. Dimensions use logical pixels; renderer scaling applies once.
+These two examples change appearance without rebuilding Plymouth.
+
+Dark:
+
+```ini
+[OnScreenKeyboard]
+BackgroundColor=0x23262e
+KeyColor=0x0e141b
+ActionColor=0x000000
+SelectedColor=0x1a9fff
+TextColor=0xffffff
+Font=Sans
+FontSize=18
+MaxWidth=1280
+HeightPercent=52
+Gap=4
+```
+
+Light:
+
+```ini
+[OnScreenKeyboard]
+BackgroundColor=0xe8e8ec
+KeyColor=0xffffff
+ActionColor=0xc8c8d0
+SelectedColor=0x99c1f1
+TextColor=0x16161b
+Font=Sans
+FontSize=20
+MaxWidth=1100
+HeightPercent=48
+Gap=6
+```
+
+The dark example gives the defaults. `FontSize` accepts 6–48, `MaxWidth`
+240–8192, `HeightPercent` 20–70 and `Gap` 0–32. Invalid values use defaults.
+Labels shrink to fit their actual key bounds. Action labels use gettext;
+character labels come from XKB, never from a translated character table.
+
+## Geometry
+
+The default geometry covers ISO key positions, including the extra key needed
+by several European layouts. Character assignment remains XKB's responsibility.
+To replace the arrangement, set `Geometry=/absolute/path/to/layout.keyboard`
+and include that file in the initramfs. Invalid geometry falls back to the
+default arrangement.
+
+Geometry uses a normal Plymouth key file. `[Keyboard Geometry]` defines
+positive `Columns` and `Rows` up to 4096. Each `[Key NAME]` group defines `X`,
+`Y`, `Width` and `Height` in those units. `NAME` is an XKB key name such as
+`AD01`, not a character such as `q`. Width and height default to four units.
+The standard geometry uses a 60 by 20 grid: four units equal one ordinary key.
+
+`Behavior` is `key` (default), `latch` for a held modifier, `lock` for a toggle
+such as Caps, or `group` to cycle layouts. A group-switch key does not need an
+XKB keycode. Geometry is limited to 128 nonoverlapping keys, all within the
+declared grid. For example, this fragment places Shift and the first two keys
+of the bottom letter row; a complete file must provide all keys its users need:
+
+```ini
+[Keyboard Geometry]
+Columns=60
+Rows=20
+
+[Key LFSH]
+X=0
+Y=12
+Width=6
+Behavior=latch
+
+[Key LSGT]
+X=6
+Y=12
+
+[Key AB01]
+X=10
+Y=12
+```
+
+Drawing and hit testing use the same computed rectangles. Changing key
+placement, spacing or overall size therefore changes the visible and touchable
+areas together.
+
+## Validation
+
+`keyboard-layout`, `keyboard` and `keyboard-geometry` are Meson tests for input,
+ownership, modifiers, composition, bounds and configuration. They complement
+boot tests; passing them does not establish touchscreen, display hotplug or
+encrypted-disk unlock behavior. The submission's test report records the boot
+configurations actually exercised and any remaining limitations.
